{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778939261,"nonce":"CEwG9IC5aNv8sQvmnakw3osALNw=","message":"## Review of the proposed spec (049671d)\n\n(Posted by Claude in a separate session at MLL's request — independent review, not a continuation of the spec-writing thread.)\n\nThe diagnosis is right and the broad shape is reasonable: typed SQLite ledger + git-bug-as-adapter + append-only event log + projections. But three category-level holes should be closed before this gets broken into child issues, plus a handful of schema-level details that look under-thought.\n\n### Most important concerns\n\n**1. Loss of git-bug's distributed property is unacknowledged.** Today, issues live in git refs and propagate across clones. This spec puts canonical state in a local SQLite file and demotes git-bug to a compatibility surface. There is no multi-device story, no backup/recovery section, and no answer for \"what happens when the SQLite DB is lost or corrupt?\" If the answer is \"git-bug export is your backup,\" that needs to be explicit — and then the export path has to be reliable enough to actually be a backup, which the spec does not argue.\n\n**2. Conflict resolution is hand-waved.** \"Mandible wins for canonical fields when locally dirty; external divergence becomes a conflict event and visible warning\" reads well but does not answer the operational question: when the export finds that the git-bug ref has moved (another machine, another agent, manual edit), what does the user actually see and do? \"Visible warning\" is not a resolution UI. Without that, \"Mandible wins\" silently overwrites, which is worse than today's lock contention because it loses data rather than blocking.\n\n**3. The migration is flip-the-switch with no dual-write/shadow phase.** Step 4 just says \"switch issue routes and CLI to ledger-backed reads/writes.\" For canonical state, the safer pattern is dual-write for a period, compare ledger vs git-bug reads, then promote ledger to authoritative once they reconcile cleanly on a real corpus. There is also no rollback story. Given that work-units, sessions, auth requests, and commit trailers all key off short IDs, a botched migration is hard to undo.\n\n**4. Scope is one umbrella's worth of design but ~5 umbrellas' worth of work.** Schema + service + import + export + sync jobs + work-unit integration + session integration + auth integration + commit/resolution backfill + graph projection rewrite + UI rebuild + SSE + CLI switch. The 8-step rollout has no validation gates between steps. This needs to be split into an umbrella with child issues (ledger core / git-bug adapter / work-unit migration / auth integration / graph rewrite / UI / cleanup) so each layer is independently reviewable and revertable. The graph + UI rewrite probably belong in a separate umbrella that follows once the ledger is stable; the \"avoid another graph refactor later\" argument cuts both ways — bundling makes the change unmergeable.\n\n### Schema-level issues\n\n- `status CHECK(status IN ('open','closed'))` is binary, but `work_units` already uses `open/committed/resolved/abandoned`, and the spec adds `resolution`, `priority`, `kind` without enumeration. Either align with the rest of the system or justify the divergence.\n- `sync_state`, `source`, `priority`, `kind`, `relationship_class` are all `TEXT` with no enumeration. Decide legal values now or you will end up with inconsistent strings within a month.\n- `relationship_type` vs `relationship_class` are two type-like columns with no clear taxonomy. Pick one or define both crisply.\n- `ledger_issue_aliases` PK includes nullable `repo_id`. SQLite NULL-in-PK semantics are surprising; this will bite during dedup.\n- No FK constraints anywhere. Intentional? With canonical state moving here, referential integrity matters more, not less.\n- Identity format is unspecified. UUIDv7? Content hash like git-bug? This affects sort order, debuggability, and whether two clones can ever produce the same ID for the same content.\n\n### Missing entirely\n\n- Backup/recovery story (see #1).\n- Multi-device story. Today, multiple Mandible instances all read the same git-bug refs. Now each has its own SQLite ledger.\n- CLAUDE.md reflection. The project CLAUDE.md explicitly requires this for backend changes. The global CLAUDE.md still names git-bug as fallback, and commit-trailer conventions reference short IDs. Both likely need updates, and the spec does not call this out.\n- Performance targets. \"Slow-query problem\" is stated motivation; no benchmark goals.\n- `IssueScanner` long-term fate — \"retained during transition\" but never removed or kept.\n- Concrete plan for ambiguous short-ID backfill across repos. There will be collisions; the spec says \"UI disambiguates\" but does not specify how a `Bug: 097d2b7` trailer that matches two ledger rows is resolved during migration.\n\n### What the spec gets right\n\n- Single-transaction current-state + event-log writes, then SSE.\n- Aliases as a first-class table rather than one external ID per issue.\n- Service-module split (`ledger-store` / `ledger-events` / `git-bug-adapter` / `ledger-graph-adapter`).\n- Auth requests as issue-attached evidence in issue mode, first-class in auth/work modes — right framing for the graph.\n- Explicit non-goals section.\n- \"Decisions I recommend unless MLL disagrees\" framing — reviewable, not adopt-or-reject.\n\n### Recommended next steps\n\n1. Add three sections to the spec before splitting into children: **backup/recovery**, **conflict UX**, **migration with dual-write + reconcile**.\n2. Enumerate `status` / `source` / `sync_state` / `priority` / `kind` / `relationship_type` / `relationship_class` values in the spec itself.\n3. Decide and document the canonical ID format.\n4. File a paired dotfiles git-bug issue for the CLAUDE.md updates this implies (Issue Tracking section, commit-trailer conventions).\n5. Split into ~5 child issues under this umbrella so review and rollback work per layer.\n6. Reconsider whether the graph + UI rewrite belong in this umbrella, or as a follow-up umbrella once the ledger is stable.","files":null}]}